"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][20]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M20-L1 – Stationarity, Autocorrelation and Time-Series Splits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Recognise weakly stationary\nprocesses, interpret autocorrelation and validate forecasts in\nchronological order.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Time is part of the data<\/h2>\n<p>The hourly power of a cooling plant cannot be treated like a randomly\nordered sample. Temperatures, control mechanisms and the environment\nintroduce temporal dependencies. A process\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis weakly stationary if its expected value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand finite variance are constant and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_t,Y_{t-k})=\\gamma(k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends only on the lag\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStrict stationarity requires all finite-dimensional distributions to be\ninvariant under shifts in time. The two concepts are not equivalent. A\nstrictly stationary process with finite second moments is weakly\nstationary.<\/p>\n<p>A realisation may appear to contain long trends even though its\ngenerating process is stationary. Conversely, a short non-stationary\nseries may look unremarkable. Autocorrelation is not a causal\nexplanation; it describes linear temporal dependence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Autocorrelation and differences<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001710_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of a stationary process and a random walk with growing variability.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of a stationary process and a\nrandom walk with growing variability.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Stationary series and random-walk variance.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma(0)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(k)=\\gamma(k)/\\gamma(0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn empirical ACF may use<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\rho(k)=\\frac{\\sum_{t=k+1}^{n}(y_t-\\bar y)(y_{t-k}-\\bar y)}{\\sum_{t=1}^{n}(y_t-\\bar y)^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_t=0.7Y_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith white noise of variance 4 and stationary initialisation, we have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.7<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.843<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma(0)=4/(1-0.7^2)\\approx7.843<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0.7<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho(k)=0.7^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn contrast, the random walk\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_t=X_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas growing variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts first differences are the original noise. The diagram illustrates\nthe theoretical variances. An AR(1) series with negative feedback has\nalternating correlations; a slowly decaying ACF, however, can indicate\npersistence or non-stationarity.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>k <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rho <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>k<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.7<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(v, <span class=\"fl\">7.8431372549<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(rho[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>], <span class=\"fl\">.343<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(v, rho)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Chronologically valid training and test data<\/h2>\n<p>For a one-hour forecast at the end of hour 120, only values actually\navailable by that time may be used. Scaling, imputation and feature\nselection must also be based on the training segment. Rolling forecast\norigins recreate this scenario: for example, train through hour 120,\nforecast hours 121–124, then train through hour 124 and forecast hours\n125–128. Overlapping target windows or delayed labels may require an\nadditional gap. Conventional random splits are often optimistic\nhere.<\/p>\n<p>Unit-root tests (ADF) and stationarity tests (KPSS) have different\nnull hypotheses. Trends, seasonal changes and structural breaks can\naffect both. Differencing does not automatically improve forecasting and\ncan generate negative autocorrelation when overdone. Diagnosis, domain\nplausibility and backtesting belong together.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A stationary AR(1) process has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi=-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand innovation variance 3. Calculate its variance and ACF for the first\nthree positive lags. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3/(1-0.25)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe correlations are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.25<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-0.125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhen forecasting hours 201–204, randomly mixing the data with\nmeasurements taken after hour 200 is inadmissible.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/arima.html\">Hyndman and\nAthanasopoulos: Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd ed., Chapter\n9<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · stationarity, differencing, ARIMA and SARIMA<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/toolbox.html\">Hyndman and\nAthanasopoulos: Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd ed., Chapter\n5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · time-series validation and distributional forecasting<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M20-L2 – ARIMA and Seasonal Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Combine AR, MA and differencing\nterms and transform forecasts back to the original scale.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From lag to model equation<\/h2>\n<p>Using the backshift operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">By_t=y_{t-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nARIMA separates three tasks: dynamic feedback, differencing and the\nprocessing of earlier innovation shocks. Under a common sign convention,\nARIMA<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(p,d,q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis given by<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(B)(1-B)^dy_t=c+\\theta(B)\\varepsilon_t,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(B)=1-\\phi_1B-\\cdots-\\phi_pB^p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta(B)=1+\\theta_1B+\\cdots+\\theta_qB^q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn MA term is therefore not a moving average of the data. The innovation\nsequence has zero expectation and constant variance; normally\ndistributed forecast intervals require additional distributional\nassumptions. The roots of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutside the unit circle ensure stationarity of the AR component; the\ncorresponding property of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nensures invertibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursion on the original scale<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001508_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Recursive point forecasts over three horizons with corresponding increments.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Recursive point forecasts over three\nhorizons with corresponding increments.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Recursive ARIMA forecast.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Suppose the latest meter reading is 120 units and its last increment\nwas four units. The model for increments is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t=2+0.5(z_{t-1}-2)+\\varepsilon_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_t=y_t-y_{t-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is an ARIMA(1,1,0) with drift (mean increment 2). Assuming that\nfuture innovations have mean zero, the next expected increments are 3,\n2.5 and 2.25. On the original scale these correspond to 123, 125.5 and\n127.75. Forecasting only the differences would not suffice for planning.\nThe diagram shows both quantities on a common time axis but with\nseparate unit ranges.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>h<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.cumsum(z)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(z,[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.25<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(y,[<span class=\"dv\">123<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">125.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">127.75<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(z,y)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Seasonal structure and model selection<\/h2>\n<p>For quarterly calendar data, the seasonal period can be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA multiplicative SARIMA model extends the equation to<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(B)\\Phi(B^s)(1-B)^d(1-B^s)^D y_t=c+\\theta(B)\\Theta(B^s)\\varepsilon_t.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The seasonal difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t-y_{t-s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBefore estimating further terms, check that the seasonal period is\nmeaningful in the domain and stable; movable holidays may require\nregressors instead of pure periodicity. ACF/PACF provide starting points\nfor model orders, and information criteria such as AICc support\nselection for comparable likelihoods (particularly with the same\ndifferencing). Check residuals for remaining dependence, for example\nwith the Ljung–Box test; a non-significant test does not prove the\nmodel. Compare against naïve and seasonal naïve forecasts in\nbacktesting. Future values of exogenous regressors must actually be\navailable or forecast themselves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>The latest meter reading is 90 and the last increment is 0. The same\nmodel yields increments of 1 and 1.5, hence forecasts of 91 and 92.5.\nFor a seasonal naïve quarterly model whose latest observed quarters are\n7, 10, 9, 12, the next four forecasts are 7, 10, 9, 12.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/arima.html\">Hyndman and\nAthanasopoulos: Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd ed., Chapter\n9<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · stationarity, differencing, ARIMA and SARIMA<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M20-L3 – State-Space Models and Kalman Filters<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Separate hidden system states\nfrom measurement errors and derive a filtering cycle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dynamics and measurement channel<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor records a slowly changing fill level. The actual fill level\nis not directly known and individual measurements are noisy. A linear\nstate-space model separates these layers:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t=F x_{t-1}+w_t,\\qquad y_t=H x_t+v_t.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the state dynamics matrix and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe observation matrix. Assume the disturbances\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_t,v_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave zero mean, are independent over time and mutually independent;\ntheir covariances are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder these assumptions, Gaussian initial states and disturbances yield\nexactly Gaussian conditional states. In other linear cases, under\nsuitable second-moment assumptions, the Kalman filter minimises mean\nsquared error within the class of linear estimators.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Predicting and correcting<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001602_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Predicted and updated state distributions after a new observation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Predicted and updated state distributions\nafter a new observation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: State-density update in the Kalman filter.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>From the last filtered mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand covariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfirst obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^-=Fm<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=FPF^\\top+Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe innovation covariance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=HP^-H^\\top+R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand for invertible\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi>H<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=P^-H^\\top S^{-1},\\quad m^+=m^-+K(y-Hm^-).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The Joseph form of the updated covariance is numerically useful:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>K<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^+=(I-KH)P^-(I-KH)^\\top+KRK^\\top.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the scalar fill-level model, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=H=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBefore measurement,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P^-=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1.5/3.5=3/7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe corrected estimate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11.286<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10+9/7\\approx11.286<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand its variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.857<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">6/7\\approx0.857<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration depicts the two Gaussian state estimates before and\nafter measurement; the measurement itself is at 13.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m,P,Q,R,y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">10.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">13.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>P0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>P<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>Q<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>K<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>P0<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(P0<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>R)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>post<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>m<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>var<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>K)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>P0<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>K<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>R<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose([K,post,var],[<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">79<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(K,post,var)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Filters, smoothers and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Filtering uses only measurements up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nsmoothing also uses subsequent measurements and is therefore not a\nreal-time forecast. Forecasting advances only the state step. If a\nmeasurement is missing, the correction step is omitted; an observed zero\nis a different matter. Non-linear dynamics can be handled by methods\nsuch as extended and unscented Kalman filters or particle filters;\nmultimodal states often cannot be represented adequately by a single\nGaussian state.<\/p>\n<p>When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q,R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare unknown, their estimation must be separated from subsequent\nevaluation. Innovation residuals and their modelled dispersion help\ndetect poorly calibrated filters. In multidimensional systems,\nobservability must also be examined, for example using the rank of the\nstacked matrices\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H,HF,\\ldots,HF^{n-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA numerically stable implementation solves linear systems rather than\nexplicitly inverting matrices.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>If the measurement variance is increased to 6,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1.5/7.5=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe new mean is 10.6 and the variance 1.2. The model places less trust\nin the measurement. If the measurement is missing entirely, the mean\nremains 10 and the variance 1.5 at this time step.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://homepages.inf.ed.ac.uk/rbf/CVonline/LOCAL_COPIES/WELCH/kalman.1.html\">Welch\nand Bishop: An Introduction to the Kalman Filter, Section 1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\ndiscrete Kalman filter<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M20-L4 – Spectral Analysis and Time-Dependent Features<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine frequency components\nfrom discrete signals and avoid aliasing and data leakage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Frequency rather than time<\/h2>\n<p>A motor generates vibrations. Several superimposed periods may be\ndifficult to distinguish from the time trace alone. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequally spaced samples\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe discrete Fourier transform calculates<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_k=\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x_n\\exp(-2\\pi i kn/N).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At sampling rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hertz, the frequency spacing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s/N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor real signals, the magnitudes at positive and negative frequencies\nare mirror images. Without suitable band-limiting, frequencies above\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot be uniquely reconstructed (aliasing). The FFT is an efficient\nalgorithm for the same transform; it is not itself a model of the cause\nof a peak.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A synthetic vibration signal<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001708_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Frequency spectrum identifies two synthetic oscillatory components.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Frequency spectrum identifies two\nsynthetic oscillatory components.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Spectrum of two oscillations.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We measure four seconds at 64 Hz. The signal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=2\\sin(2\\pi\\cdot5t)+0.5\\sin(2\\pi\\cdot12t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontains 256 samples. Both frequencies fall exactly on grid points. With\nan unweighted window and a one-sided power spectrum, the powers at the\ntwo frequencies are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^2/2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>0.5<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5^2/2=0.125<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe unit is the squared signal unit; here the sum equals the variance of\n2.125. A power spectral density (PSD), by contrast, is measured in\nsignal units squared per hertz. The diagram shows the two peaks of the\nself-generated signal.<\/p>\n<p>When the window contains a non-integer number of periods, energy\nspreads across neighbouring frequencies. Window functions such as Hann\ncan suppress sidelobes but broaden the main lobe. Zero-padding provides\na denser frequency grid; it does not improve the resolving power limited\nby measurement duration and window choice.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.signal <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> periodogram<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>fs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">64<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(<span class=\"dv\">256<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>fs<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sin(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>f,p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>periodogram(x,fs<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>fs,scaling<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;spectrum&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(p[[<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">48<\/span>]],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.125<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(p.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(),<span class=\"fl\">2.125<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(f[np.argsort(p)[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:]],p[[<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">48<\/span>]])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Features for temporally valid models<\/h2>\n<p>Typical features include band energy, dominant frequency, moving\naverages, variance and temporal gradients. Welch estimation averages\nmodified periodograms over segments; when rotational speed varies,\ntime-local methods (STFT, wavelets) may be useful. For a warning at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe window may extend only up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA centred window would use future measurements. A machine-state\nclassifier should preferably be validated by machine and time block,\nrather than by randomly mixing heavily overlapping windows. With\nirregular sampling, the ordinary FFT grid is not directly applicable;\nfor example, a Lomb–Scargle periodogram may be used.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>At 80 Hz and 400 samples, the grid spacing is 0.2 Hz; the Nyquist\nfrequency is 40 Hz. Without an anti-aliasing filter, an isolated\nsinusoid at 53 Hz appears at 27 Hz (with the corresponding phase/sign\nchange). Distinguishing these cases requires additional knowledge or\nfaster sampling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.periodogram.html\">SciPy:\nperiodogram<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · spectral density, frequencies and scaling<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M20-L5 – Spatial Dependence, Variograms and Kriging<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret a semivariogram and\ncalculate ordinary kriging as a linear prediction problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distance and direction as model information<\/h2>\n<p>Soil moisture at two neighbouring measurement sites is often more\nsimilar than at distant sites. The observational unit is now a location\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndistances must be appropriate to the scale and coordinate system. Under\nintrinsic stationarity, increments have zero expectation and the\nvariance of the difference depends only on the displacement vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe semivariogram is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma(h)=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Var}[Z(s+h)-Z(s)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Under second-order stationarity with covariance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(h)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma(h)=C(0)-C(h)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEmpirically, squared differences between pairs of observations are\ngrouped into distance classes and divided by twice the number of pairs.\nUnder isotropy, only distance matters. A directional variogram can\nreveal anisotropy. A nugget describes a jump at small positive\ndistances, for example due to measurement noise or microscale variation.\nA sill is a long-term plateau; the range characterises the distance at\nwhich spatial dependence practically or exactly dies away, depending on\nthe model family.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Weighted prediction at a new location<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001617_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Spatial measurement sites and a variogram used to estimate the centre.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Spatial measurement sites and a variogram\nused to estimate the centre.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Kriging and variogram.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Ordinary kriging estimates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>Z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat Z(s_0)=\\sum_i w_i Z(s_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a constant unknown mean and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_iw_i=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a valid covariance matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand covariance vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the target location, we solve<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>C<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>w<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix} C&amp;\\mathbf{1}\\\\\\mathbf{1}^\\top&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}w\\\\\\lambda\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}c\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This equation minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>C<\/mi><mi>w<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(0)-2w^\\top c+w^\\top Cw<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubject to the sum constraint. Two measurement sites are located at 0\nand 2 km, with moisture values of 20 and 28 percentage points. For a\ntarget at 1 km and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(h)=4\\exp(-|h|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin squared percentage points (h expressed numerically in km), both\ncovariances with the target are equal. By symmetry,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w=(0.5,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the estimate is 24 percentage points. The prediction variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.328<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4-8e^{-1}+2+2e^{-2}\\approx3.328<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsquared percentage points. The illustration shows the target and\nmeasurement sites as well as the semivariogram derived from the chosen\ncovariance.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">20.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">28.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> s0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>C<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(s[:,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s[<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>,:]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s0))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.block([[C,np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))],[np.ones((<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)),np.zeros((<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.solve(A,np.r_[c,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>])[:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>variance<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>C<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>w<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(w,[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(w<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>z,<span class=\"dv\">24<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(variance,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.e<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.e<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(w, w<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>z,variance)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Spatial validation and model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Kriging weights need not be positive. A plausible variogram is not\nnecessarily a valid covariance model; established admissible model\nfamilies should be used in practice. With a spatial trend, universal\nkriging with trend regressors may be more appropriate. Gaussian process\nmodels from M18 offer a related probabilistic approach. The kriging\nvariance assumes the covariance model is known and may therefore\nunderestimate the additional uncertainty of parameter estimation.<\/p>\n<p>For prediction in unmeasured regions, spatial block cross-validation\nis usually more informative than randomly splitting nearby measurement\npoints. The variogram and trend are re-estimated for each training\nblock. For measurement networks with unequal spacing, spatial barriers\nor multiple scales, the distance definition must be justified\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Replace the moisture values with 18 and 32 percentage points. With\nunchanged locations and a known covariance model, the weights and\nvariance remain the same; the prediction increases to 25 percentage\npoints. In a real application, refitting the covariance model after\nchanging the values may also alter the weights.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://courses.ems.psu.edu/geog586/node/683\">Penn State:\nGEOG 586, A Statistical Approach to Interpolation: Kriging<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nsemivariogram and spatial interpolation<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www2.imm.dtu.dk/pubdb/pubs/5177-full.html\">Geostatistics\nand Analysis of Spatial Data, DTU<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · kriging and covariance\nmodels<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M20-L6 – Functional Data and Curve Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model curves as observations,\nsmooth them with basis functions and interpret functional principal\ncomponents.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The observation is a function<\/h2>\n<p>Three test rigs each record a temperature trace during a load test.\nNot every time point corresponds to an independent object. Rather, each\ncomplete curve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an observation, and discrete values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_{ij}=x_i(t_{ij})+\\varepsilon_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare measurements of the same function. Curves may be recorded on\ndifferent time grids. Smoothing replaces noise with a structured\nestimate; interpolation without smoothing would interpret disturbances\nas signal as well.<\/p>\n<p>A basis expansion uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>K<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i(t)\\approx\\sum_{k=1}^K a_{ik}b_k(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example B-splines, Fourier functions or polynomials. With design\nmatrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{jk}=b_k(t_j)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand curvature penalty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><msubsup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msubsup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{kl}=\\int b_k&#39;&#39;(t)b_l&#39;&#39;(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe solve<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>R<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat a=\\arg\\min_a\\{\\|y-Ba\\|_2^2+\\lambda a^\\top Ra\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B^\\top B+\\lambda R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis invertible, the solution follows from a linear system. The penalty is\nsemidefinite: linear functions are not penalised by the second\nderivative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A smoothing example on normalised time<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001618_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of observed curves, basis representation and smoothing penalty.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of observed curves, basis\nrepresentation and smoothing penalty.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Functional basis and curvature penalty.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>On\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[0,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nchoose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b(t)=(1,t,t^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\operatorname{diag}(0,0,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe measurements at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,0.5,1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare 20, 23 and 20 degrees Celsius. Without a penalty, the model\ninterpolates with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=(20,12,-12)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1/16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>146<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=(146/7,12/7,-12/7)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(rounded to 20.857; 1.714; −1.714). The diagram shows the attenuated\npeak: the strongly curved interpolation becomes a flat curve. The large\nsmoothing effect here follows from the presence of only three\nmeasurement points and the chosen penalty. It is not a recommendation\nfor choosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin other problems. Too large a penalty can remove genuine process\npeaks.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>t<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">20.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">23.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">20.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>B<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.column_stack([np.ones(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),t,t<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>t])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> R<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.solve(B,y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.linalg.solve(B.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>B<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>R<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>,B.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(a0,[<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># Erwartungswerte durch direkte Substitution in die Normalgleichung<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose((B.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>B<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>R<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>a,B.T<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>y)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(a,[<span class=\"dv\">146<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a0,a,B<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>a)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Functional PCA and regression<\/h2>\n<p>The mean curve is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu(t)=n^{-1}\\sum_i x_i(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe covariance kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G(s,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes joint changes. Functional PCA seeks orthonormal eigenfunctions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int G(s,t)\\psi_j(t)\\,dt=\\eta_j\\psi_j(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand scores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\xi_{ij}=\\int(x_i(t)-\\mu(t))\\psi_j(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn a discrete grid, quadrature must be taken into account in the\nnormalisation. With a non-orthogonal basis, the Gram matrix of the basis\nfunctions plays a role. This distinguishes curve analysis from\nunweighted PCA of arbitrarily sampled columns.<\/p>\n<p>In scalar-on-function regression, for example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y_i\\mid x_i]=\\alpha+\\int\\beta(t)x_i(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBecause the inputs are high-dimensional and correlated,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso requires structure or regularisation. When predicting from partial\ncurves, smoothing must not use future measurement points. Differences in\ntiming (phase) and magnitude (amplitude) may require separate\nregistration. Validation is performed by complete independent curves or\ntest rigs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What function results when the curvature penalty is increased\nindefinitely for these data? Solution: The quadratic component tends to\nzero. Because the measurement points are symmetric, the best linear fit\nis the constant 21 degrees Celsius. For unequal time intervals, a\ndiscrete approximation of the functional inner product should use\nappropriate quadrature weights.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/bimj.202300363\">Gertheiss\net al. (2024): Functional Data Analysis: An Introduction and Recent\nDevelopments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · smoothing, basis functions and FPCA<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M20-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M20-L7 – Prediction Intervals and Backtesting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Time Series, Spatial and Functional Data<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective/methods<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M17, M18, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate horizon-dependent\nuncertainty and evaluate point and interval forecasts on chronologically\nseparated data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>An interval for future observations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001669_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Predictive intervals widening with forecast horizon.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Predictive intervals widening with\nforecast horizon.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Random-walk forecast fan.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A warehouse needs not only an expected value for demand, but also\ninformation about how far demand may deviate from it. A prediction\ninterval describes possible future observations; a confidence interval\nfor an estimated mean answers a different question. For a simple random\nwalk\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_t=y_{t-1}+\\varepsilon_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith independent innovations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(0,\\sigma^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand known\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconditional on the past we have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∼<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{T+h}\\mid Y_T=y_T\\sim N(y_T,h\\sigma^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_T=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe symmetric 95% intervals are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1.96<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msqrt><mi>h<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\pm1.96\\cdot3\\sqrt h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(rounded normal quantile). At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis gives [94.12; 105.88], and at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n[88.24; 111.76]. The diagram shows dispersion growing with the square\nroot of the horizon. Drift, parameter uncertainty or non-normally\ndistributed shocks might require different intervals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Looking back under realistic conditions<\/h2>\n<p>A backtest consists of a specified training window, a forecast origin\nand the horizon to be examined. At each origin, all processing steps use\nonly admissible historical information. As an illustration, we evaluate\nfour values forecast at the same horizon: observations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>102<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>95<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>110<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>103<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(102,95,110,103)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npoint forecasts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>104<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>105<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(100,100,104,105)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlower bounds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>96<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>99<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>101<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(96,96,99,101)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand upper bounds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>104<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>104<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>109<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>109<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(104,104,109,109)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe absolute errors are 2, 5, 6 and 2; the MAE is 3.75. Two of the four\nobservations fall within the intervals, giving empirical coverage of 50%\nand a mean width of 8.5. Four cases are insufficient for a reliable\nconclusion about calibration.<\/p>\n<p>An interval-based measure penalises both width and non-coverage. For\na two-sided interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[l,u]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith nominal coverage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe use<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">IS_\\alpha=(u-l)+\\frac{2}{\\alpha}(l-y)\\mathbf{1}_{y&lt;l}+\\frac{2}{\\alpha}(y-u)\\mathbf{1}_{y&gt;u}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe example yields scores of 8, 28, 30 and 8, with a mean of 18.5.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">102<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">95<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">110<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">103<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> pred<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">104<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">105<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>l<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">96<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">96<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">99<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">101<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">104<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">104<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">109<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">109<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mae<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.mean(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>pred))<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> cover<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.mean((y<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span>l)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>u))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>score<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>l<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">20<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.maximum(l<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">20<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.maximum(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>u,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(mae,<span class=\"fl\">3.75<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(cover,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(np.mean(u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>l),<span class=\"fl\">8.5<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(score.mean(),<span class=\"fl\">18.5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mae,cover,np.mean(u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>l),score,score.mean())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calibration, horizons and model selection<\/h2>\n<p>Coverage and width must be evaluated together: arbitrarily wide\nintervals do not solve a planning problem. Summaries by horizon, region\nor season show whether deviations occur systematically. Pinball loss\nevaluates individual quantiles, while CRPS evaluates entire predictive\ndistributions. Block bootstrap or simulation from a fitted model can\naccount for dependencies; unexamined i.i.d. resampling of individual\ntime points is problematic under autocorrelation. Conformal methods\nsuitable for time series require explicitly appropriate assumptions or\nadaptations; ordinary i.i.d. coverage guarantees cannot simply be\ntransferred to drift.<\/p>\n<p>Models are selected using rolling validation windows and finally\ntested on a time span not used for selection. Forecast errors are\ndependent when horizons overlap; simple standard errors based on\nindependent cases are then inappropriate. For an inventory planning\ndecision, asymmetric error costs may be more relevant than RMSE\nalone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>How wide is the random walk’s 95% interval after nine steps?\nSolution: The standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><msqrt><mn>9<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\sqrt9=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe half-width is 17.64 and the full width is 35.28. An interval with\n90% nominal coverage and bounds [92,108] for an observation of 111\nreceives a score of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>76<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16+20\\cdot3=76<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/arima.html\">Hyndman and\nAthanasopoulos: Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd ed., Chapter\n9<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · stationarity, differencing, ARIMA and SARIMA<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://otexts.com/fpp3/toolbox.html\">Hyndman and\nAthanasopoulos: Forecasting: Principles and Practice, 3rd ed., Chapter\n5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · time-series validation and distributional forecasting<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
